[TEST_CASE]
UID: TP-UCSI-004
TITLE: Standalone fault clearing
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify that Clear Faults clears active system faults from the standalone UCSI application and remains unavailable from the hosted UCSI module.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
Bench test setup with the GCB communication channel available and an approved procedure for safely inducing an active system fault.

The standalone UCSI application and the system PC application containing the hosted UCSI module are installed.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Safely induce an active system fault, start the standalone UCSI application, open the Faults view, and confirm that the fault is displayed.
2. While monitoring live telemetry and not recording, confirm that Clear Faults is enabled.
3. Start a telemetry recording and confirm that Clear Faults is disabled, then stop recording. Load a recorded session and confirm that Clear Faults remains disabled during replay, then return to live monitoring.
4. Invoke Clear Faults and confirm that the active fault clears.
5. Safely induce an active system fault again, close the standalone application, start the system PC application, and open the hosted UCSI module's Faults view.
6. Confirm that Clear Faults is disabled and that the active fault remains present.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
In standalone live, non-recording operation, Clear Faults is enabled and sends the clear-faults command to the GCB, which clears the active system fault. The command is disabled during recording and replay. In the hosted UCSI module, Clear Faults remains disabled and does not clear the active fault.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-UCSI-008
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TP-UCSI-005
TITLE: HVPS fault-status bit mapping
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify that UCSI decodes and displays each named HVPS fault indicator from its
assigned HVPS status bit rather than from the HVPS IO field.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
Bench test setup with a GCB telemetry source that can independently control the
HVPS status and IO fields.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Start UCSI, connect the bench test setup, and open the HVPS-state view.
2. For each mapping below, publish telemetry with only the specified HVPS status
   bit set and with the HVPS IO field clear:

   * bit 10 - Filament Clock Fault;
   * bit 11 - Cathode Arc;
   * bit 12 - Fan Fault;
   * bit 13 - 24V Overcurrent Fault;
   * bit 14 - Master Fault;
   * bit 15 - HV Overcurrent Fault;
   * bit 16 - Temperature 1 Fault;
   * bit 17 - Cathode Overcurrent Fault;
   * bit 18 - Temperature 3 Fault; and
   * bit 19 - Temperature 2 Fault.

3. For each sample, confirm that the corresponding named indicator is active
   and displays ``On`` while the other nine named fault indicators are inactive
   and display ``Off``.
4. Publish telemetry with HVPS status bits 10 through 19 clear and confirm that
   all ten named fault indicators are inactive and display ``Off``.
5. Keep the HVPS status field clear. Set HVPS IO bits 1, 5, 6, and 10 through 16
   individually and confirm that none of the ten named fault indicators becomes
   active.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
Each HVPS status bit activates only its assigned named indicator and clearing
the bit deactivates that indicator. The tested HVPS IO bits do not activate any
of the ten indicators.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-UCSI-009
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TP-UCSI-006
TITLE: Telemetry-gated service and one-point test emission
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify periodic GCB communication, live-telemetry gating of service commands,
one-point emission parameter limits, telemetry-confirmed sequencing, and stop
behavior.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
Bench test setup with a GCB communication simulator that can publish selected
control states, suspend telemetry, replay a recorded session, delay command
responses, and record every command without energizing X-ray output.

UCSI is installed, and qualified personnel have completed the approved
radiation-safety procedure for service/test emission commands.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Start UCSI and record the GCB requests for at least three seconds. Confirm
   that one version-information request occurs each second with no duplicate
   request loop when the UCSI view is activated repeatedly.
2. Make the simulator withhold version-information responses for longer than
   500 ms over several request intervals. Confirm that the first consecutive
   failure produces one warning and that a subsequent successful response
   allows the first failure of a later failure run to produce one new warning.
3. Publish live ``Cold`` telemetry, command one permitted direct HVPS value and
   one permitted coil value, and confirm that both commands are recorded.
   Suspend telemetry for more than 1.5 seconds and repeat the commands; then
   load a recorded session and repeat them again. Confirm that no command is
   recorded for either unavailable condition. Return to live telemetry,
   publish ``Ready``, repeat the direct-service commands, and confirm that
   neither is recorded.
4. In the Emission view, enter values at the configured inclusive limits for acceleration
   potential, power, filament current, duration, and each coil current. Confirm that every
   boundary combination is accepted.
5. In turn, enter a value immediately outside each configured limit and a zero or
   non-finite value where the parameter must be positive. Attempt to run each case and
   confirm that no emission-sequence command is recorded.
6. With valid parameters and fresh live ``Cold`` telemetry, start an emission.
   For each recorded command, hold the current telemetry state briefly and
   confirm that the next state-dependent command is not sent until the
   simulator publishes the required state. Confirm this order: reset timers;
   clear plan; warmup; ``Primed``; new session; ``Staging``; load point; stage
   plan; ``Staged``; confirm point; release plan; ``Ready``; release point; and
   ``Emission``.
7. Repeat step 6 from ``Primed`` and confirm that reset, clear-plan, and warmup
   commands are skipped. Repeat from ``Staged`` and confirm that UCSI first
   sends Stop and waits for ``Cold`` before executing the full sequence.
8. During a sequence wait, suspend telemetry for more than 1.5 seconds and
   confirm that the sequence fails without sending the next command. Restore
   fresh telemetry, start another sequence, invoke Stop while it is in
   progress, and confirm cancellation followed by one Stop command. Publish a
   normal active state, invoke Stop again, and confirm one Stop command.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
UCSI maintains one once-per-second version-information request loop with a
500 ms response timeout and suppresses repeated warnings during one continuous
failure. Operator-issued service commands require fresh live telemetry and a
permitted control state. One-point parameters within the configured service limits follow
the state-confirmed command sequence from each supported start state, while values outside
those limits, replay, stale or missing telemetry, and non-permitted states do not
transmit those commands. Loss of fresh telemetry stops sequence advancement,
and Stop cancels an in-progress sequence or stops a normal active state.
<<<
